{"qid":"Q12844800","names":["Ghaznavid Empire"],"title":"Ghaznavid Empire","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Ghaznavid Empire grew from the governorship that Sabuktigin, a Turkish slave-soldier, established at Ghazna in Afghanistan under Samanid suzerainty in 977; his son Mahmud (r. 998–1030) broke free of Samanid oversight and built the greatest territorial extent of the dynasty — ruling Khorasan, eastern Iran, Afghanistan, and launching seventeen celebrated raids into the Indian subcontinent that carried off immense wealth, destroyed the Somnath temple in 1026, and introduced Persianate Islam into the Punjab. Mahmud's court at Ghazna was among the most brilliant of the medieval Islamic world, patronizing Firdausi, Biruni, and Utbi. The Ghaznavids lost their Iranian and Central Asian territories to the Seljuq Turks after the decisive Battle of Dandanaqan in 1040 and retreated to Lahore and the Punjab, where a much-reduced dynasty survived until the Ghurid conquest of Ghazna in 1151 and Lahore in 1186.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Ghaznavid core territories in Khorasan, Afghanistan, and the Punjab are attested by administrative sources, the chronicles of Gardizi and Baihaqi, and coin finds; the extent of tribute-paying frontier zones in Sind and the Gangetic plain after Mahmud's raids is less precisely bounded and relies on campaign narratives.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Ghaznavid dynasty","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Ghaznavid-dynasty"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Ghaznavids","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghaznavids"}],"britannica":"topic/Ghaznavid-dynasty","cowCode":nul